     "description": "Retired into apply_ruleset with the extract-contract ruleset. Experiment 115 read this widget operation by operation on 2026-10-15 and found that NONE OF ITS FIVE OPERATIONS IS ABOUT SEA LEVEL: four are assertions about a table -- its key columns, its unit, its provenance, a column's closed vocabulary -- and one derives a column's distinct values. Every piece of sea-level knowledge it held was in its parameters, and its code was general machinery with that knowledge compiled into it.\\n\\nWhere each operation went, all of it declared data: the six key columns and the two units to inputContract.tableContract; the family pair to its closedVocabulary; the baseline period, the basis kind and the recorded millimetre-to-metre conversion to tableContract.declares; the dataset DOI and the three obligatory citations to basis.evidence, where they resolve; and the distinct site_id values to a knob on the table input, which is P3 -- the constraint lives where the data enters, so a practitioner meets it while they can still fix the file.\\n\\nProvenance was a refusal wearing a type check: readExtract rejected a plain table because 'the dataset, baseline and citations must be declared first', and the .projection bag it demanded was a token meaning the contract was satisfied. The contract is declared now, pinned by id AND version, which makes 'values are not comparable across dataset versions' true by construction. A workflow saved before today still runs: the assignment step reads the annotated value when it is present.\\n\\nThe practitioner's standard for this, 2026-10-26: 'if another climate model ended up as a ruleset in FW it should be able to use the standard ruleset widget to express and implement its contract' -- and 'no variants'. This widget was the variant, and while it shipped it was a working example teaching the next author to make another one.",
