      "note": "THIS CHECK CHANGED THE WIDGET'S DOCUMENTATION BEFORE IT SHIPPED, which is the strongest thing a lab check has done here. Fieldwork explained the 8.40-against-5.35 gap in the South London project as rate-versus-risk. The fixture refutes it: a risk ratio over the same houses is 8.401853429780243, the rate ratio's value BIT FOR BIT, because deaths per house is a risk when a house can hold at most one death and the two are the same division. Only the intervals differ, and only in the second decimal -- 6.8410 to 10.3189 from the Poisson form against 6.8450 to 10.3128 from Katz. So the gap is the DENOMINATOR and not the estimand, and the hazard is worse than first stated: a reader who takes 8.40 for the risk ratio beside the houses table is right, and is thereby taught that the substitution is safe, which holds only while the outcome is rare. Both tables are carried in one fixture because the comparison between them IS the finding. Agreement here says nothing about agreement with another package, which this project does not claim. UPDATED 2026-10-26: the labels were read, on the practitioner's pointer that they could be deduced from a results panel, the closest available source. Transcribed from a classic two-by-two estimate table: Risk Ratio (RR), Odds Ratio (cross product) -- the qualifier kept, since the panel prints a conditional-MLE odds ratio beside it that is not computed here -- and Risk Difference (RD%), which that panel prints as a PERCENTAGE where this carries a proportion. So criterion 5 inverted: from 'the label must be null' to 'the label must be present and must name its source, and the source must not be a classic analysis package'. What it polices moved from the absence to the provenance, which is the same purpose. Rewritten 2026-11-26 on two-by-two-snow-002, which Fieldwork makes from the people workflow itself: the first fixture was made by a chain that is gone, under label fields since renamed. The houses comparison is the rate layout no