      "note": "WHAT IS INDEPENDENT HERE IS NOT THE NORMALISATION, and the check says so rather than pretending otherwise. `normaliseName` is this project's own; a second copy written by the same author is not a second opinion, which is the trap check 27's record names. So three separable things are recomputed. The merge given keys is pandas' and fully independent. The raw-string baseline needs no normalisation at all. The folding is checked as a PROPERTY -- every folded key equals its name's alphanumeric reduction and no two distinct names fold together -- which is weaker than an independent fold and still refuses one that expanded St. to Saint, dropped a word, folded two names together or touched a letter.\n\nAND THE DATA CANNOT SUPPLY A NEGATIVE CONTROL, which is the finding worth carrying. Snow's table and these boundaries have no pair differing by more than punctuation, so nothing in the example tests a name that OUGHT not to match -- 32 of 32 is also the score of a rule that matched too readily. One part is renamed to Camberwell Green, a real place the table does not carry. Jaro-Winkler to the name it replaced is 0.925 by rapidfuzz and 0.925 by jellyfish, so a similarity-based linkage at the usual 0.90 threshold would have ACCEPTED it. This widget has no threshold and no review band and reports the part unmatched; the two figures are what that decision is worth, and are reported rather than asserted, in the role the score interval plays in check 27. Rerun 2026-11-26 at 0.7.0: the check counted shared PARTS, where Fieldwork since 0.3.1 counts units, and disagreed at 0.200.0 on the Streatham pair alone. The fixture now declares the rule.",
